1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,831
Welcome to the Modern Java Enterprise Handbook.

2
00:00:03,831 --> 00:00:07,405
This is chapter forty-two: Mockito and test doubles.

3
00:00:07,855 --> 00:00:14,125
Most real classes have collaborators: a payment gateway, a warehouse API, a database.

4
00:00:14,125 --> 00:00:18,477
Test doubles let you test a class's own logic without them.

5
00:00:18,477 --> 00:00:20,469
Used well, they're precise.

6
00:00:20,469 --> 00:00:25,263
Used everywhere, they produce a green suite that catches nothing.

7
00:00:26,163 --> 00:00:28,323
OrderService confirms an order.

8
00:00:28,323 --> 00:00:34,174
It reserves stock, charges the customer, saves the order, and emails a confirmation.

9
00:00:34,174 --> 00:00:40,862
And there's one rule that really matters: if payment fails, the reserved stock must be released.

10
00:00:41,312 --> 00:00:47,978
Testing it for real means a warehouse API, a payment gateway, a database and a mail server.

11
00:00:47,978 --> 00:00:51,860
Slow, flaky, and for the payment, actually expensive.

12
00:00:51,860 --> 00:00:57,867
A test double stands in for each collaborator, so you can test this class's logic.

13
00:00:58,767 --> 00:01:01,011
There are five kinds of double.

14
00:01:01,011 --> 00:01:03,256
A dummy just fills a parameter.

15
00:01:03,256 --> 00:01:05,428
A stub returns canned answers.

16
00:01:05,428 --> 00:01:09,193
A spy is a real object with some methods overridden.

17
00:01:09,193 --> 00:01:12,162
A mock is a stub you also verify against.

18
00:01:12,162 --> 00:01:18,027
And a fake is a simplified, working implementation, like an in-memory repository.

19
00:01:18,027 --> 00:01:21,140
Mockito's mock covers the first four roles.

20
00:01:21,140 --> 00:01:23,023
Fakes, you write yourself.

21
00:01:23,473 --> 00:01:26,885
Prefer state verification: assert on the result.

22
00:01:26,885 --> 00:01:33,566
Verifying interactions pins the test to how the code works, so harmless refactoring breaks it.

23
00:01:33,566 --> 00:01:40,390
Verify is for effects that are invisible from the outside, like an email sent or stock released.

24
00:01:41,290 --> 00:01:42,777
Here's the happy path.

25
00:01:42,777 --> 00:01:49,403
The Mockito extension creates a mock for each field, and InjectMocks builds the service with them.

26
00:01:49,403 --> 00:01:54,204
Stub stock reservation to succeed and the charge to return a reference.

27
00:01:54,204 --> 00:02:02,858
The save stub uses thenAnswer to echo back its argument, a common idiom, because repositories usually return what they're given.

28
00:02:02,858 --> 00:02:05,495
Then confirm, and assert on the result.

29
00:02:05,495 --> 00:02:07,253
That's state verification.

30
00:02:08,153 --> 00:02:09,946
Now the rule that matters.

31
00:02:09,946 --> 00:02:12,635
Stock is reserved, payment is declined.

32
00:02:12,635 --> 00:02:14,497
Assert that confirm throws.

33
00:02:14,497 --> 00:02:20,773
Then verify that releaseAll was called, that nothing was saved, and that no email went out.

34
00:02:20,773 --> 00:02:25,670
Releasing stock returns nothing and leaves no state the caller can see.

35
00:02:25,670 --> 00:02:32,153
Verifying the interaction is the only way to assert it, and that's exactly what verify is for.

36
00:02:32,603 --> 00:02:34,464
One rule about matchers.

37
00:02:34,464 --> 00:02:38,498
If one argument uses a matcher, every argument must.

38
00:02:38,498 --> 00:02:43,618
Mixing any with a raw string throws InvalidUseOfMatchersException.

39
00:02:43,618 --> 00:02:45,403
Wrap the literal in eq.

40
00:02:46,303 --> 00:02:47,114
Three tools.

41
00:02:47,114 --> 00:02:55,502
An argument captor grabs the object the code built internally, so you can assert on it after the fact and get a proper diff.

42
00:02:55,502 --> 00:02:59,764
Better than argThat, which only reports no matching invocation.

43
00:03:00,214 --> 00:03:02,099
A spy wraps a real object.

44
00:03:02,099 --> 00:03:05,361
On a spy, always stub with doReturn dot when.

45
00:03:05,361 --> 00:03:14,931
The when dot thenReturn form calls the real method while setting up the stub, hitting the remote catalogue you were trying to avoid.

46
00:03:14,931 --> 00:03:18,411
And a spy usually means the class does too much.

47
00:03:18,861 --> 00:03:21,815
And strict stubs, the extension's default.

48
00:03:21,815 --> 00:03:27,722
An unused stub fails the test, because either the test is wrong or the code changed.

49
00:03:27,722 --> 00:03:32,152
A stub called with different arguments fails too, showing both.

50
00:03:32,152 --> 00:03:37,848
Under lenient mode it would quietly return false and you'd debug the wrong thing.

51
00:03:38,748 --> 00:03:46,269
Mockito 5 can mock static methods, but it's thread-local, and a leaked one breaks later tests intermittently.

52
00:03:46,269 --> 00:03:49,649
Mocking a static is nearly always a design smell.

53
00:03:50,099 --> 00:03:55,452
The usual culprits are the current time, random UUIDs and the system clock.

54
00:03:55,452 --> 00:03:56,879
Inject them instead.

55
00:03:56,879 --> 00:04:05,801
A Clock field is testable without static mocks, and it's better production code, because time becomes an explicit dependency.

56
00:04:06,251 --> 00:04:10,916
And for stateful collaborators with small interfaces, write a fake.

57
00:04:10,916 --> 00:04:18,020
A mocked repository needs a stub for every query and breaks when the service calls a different finder.

58
00:04:18,020 --> 00:04:22,059
A fake behaves like a repository: save something, find it.

59
00:04:22,959 --> 00:04:27,288
Under the hood, Mockito uses ByteBuddy to redefine the class.

60
00:04:27,288 --> 00:04:36,443
Since Mockito 5, the inline mock maker is the default, so final classes and static methods are mockable with no extra dependency.

61
00:04:36,443 --> 00:04:44,249
Every call goes to a handler, which returns a stubbed value if there is one, and the type's default otherwise.

62
00:04:44,699 --> 00:04:45,954
Two consequences.

63
00:04:45,954 --> 00:04:55,546
Unstubbed methods return null, zero or false, not errors, so a NullPointerException in a mocked test usually means a missing stub.

64
00:04:55,546 --> 00:05:01,080
And every invocation is recorded, which is why verify works after the fact.

65
00:05:01,080 --> 00:05:05,286
And remove mockito inline from your build: it's obsolete.

66
00:05:06,186 --> 00:05:07,463
Production rules.

67
00:05:07,463 --> 00:05:14,750
Mock at the architectural boundary: things that are slow, remote, non-deterministic or expensive.

68
00:05:14,750 --> 00:05:16,704
Prefer state verification.

69
00:05:16,704 --> 00:05:18,657
Use constructor injection.

70
00:05:18,657 --> 00:05:20,836
Write fakes for repositories.

71
00:05:20,836 --> 00:05:23,465
Inject the clock and ID generators.

72
00:05:23,465 --> 00:05:25,343
And keep strict stubs on.

73
00:05:25,793 --> 00:05:30,683
Never mock value objects like Money or an Order; construct real ones.

74
00:05:30,683 --> 00:05:37,486
Never mock types you don't own: wrap the third-party client in your own interface and mock that.

75
00:05:37,486 --> 00:05:38,974
Never use deep stubs.

76
00:05:38,974 --> 00:05:41,667
Never stub a spy with when thenReturn.

77
00:05:41,667 --> 00:05:46,060
And never call reset; it means one test is testing two things.

78
00:05:46,960 --> 00:05:47,606
To recap.

79
00:05:47,606 --> 00:05:53,205
Stub to supply inputs, and verify only the effects you can't see from outside.

80
00:05:53,205 --> 00:05:57,009
Matchers are all or nothing, and spies need doReturn.

81
00:05:57,009 --> 00:06:00,239
Strict stubs catch dead and mismatched stubs.

82
00:06:00,239 --> 00:06:05,766
And rather than mocking statics or repositories, inject time and write fakes.

83
00:06:05,766 --> 00:06:12,155
A suite where every test ends in verify has high coverage and almost no defect detection.

84
00:06:12,605 --> 00:06:17,204
Next is chapter forty-three: integration testing and Testcontainers.

85
00:06:17,204 --> 00:06:18,557
Thanks for watching.
